  45–48 DA 589-592');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 1, '1. It came to pass. [The Leaders Challenge Jesus’ Authority, Luke 20:1–8=Matt. 21:23–27=Mark 11:27–33. Major comment: Matthew.]');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 6, '6. Stone us. That is, stone us to death.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 7, '7. Could not tell. Literally, “did not know.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 9, '9. A certain man. [The Wicked Husbandmen, Luke 20:9–19=Matt. 21:33–46=Mark 12:1–12. Major comment: Matthew.]
For a long time. A detail noted only by Luke.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 11, '11. Again he sent another. Literally, “he added to send another,” a strictly Hebrew idiom carried over into the Greek (see on ch. 19:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 12, '12. Wounded. Gr. traumatizō, from the noun trauma (see on ch. 10:34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 13, '13. What shall I do? Another detail preserved only by Luke.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 16, '16. God forbid. Literally, “may it not happen,” meaning, as it were, “away with the thought.” The word “God” does not appear in the Greek. This strong exclamation was uttered when the Pharisees recognized in the parable a picture of their own fate (see COL 295).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 18, '18. Grind him to powder. Or, “crush him to pieces.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 19, '19. Chief priests. For comment see on Matt. 21:23.
The same hour. They were ready to force the issue with Jesus (see Matt. 21:46).
They perceived. Here was the reason for their immediate anger.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 20, '20. Watched. [Paying Tribute to Caesar, Luke 20:20–26=Matt. 22:15–22=Mark 12:13–17. Major comment: Matthew.]
Spies. Probably these were, in this case, theological students from the seminary in Jerusalem (see DA 601). For previous encounters between Jesus and spies commissioned by the Sanhedrin see on ch. 11:54.
Feign. Or, “pretend.”
Take hold of his words. They were “watching” for something that would hold in court, so that Jesus would not be able to escape their sinister designs against His life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 22, '22. Tribute. Gr. phoros, the annual fixed tax, levied either on property or on persons.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 24, '24. A penny. A Roman denarius (see p. 49; see on Matt. 20:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 26, '26. Could not take hold. They were unable to find a word of evidence against Jesus (see on v. 20). He slipped through their hands, and as He did so, He uttered the fundamental principle that governs the Christian’s responsibilities to civil authorities.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 27, '27. Certain of the Sadducees. [Marriage and the Resurrection, Luke 20:27–38=Matt. 22:23–33=Mark 12:18–27. Major comment: Matthew.]');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 35, '35. To obtain. That is, “to reach,” or “to attain to.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 36, '36. Children of the resurrection. A Hebrew idiomatic expression carried over into the Greek, and here parallel with the expression “the children of God.” “Children of the resurrection” means, simply, those raised from the dead. They have been given life again by the same power that originally gave them life. Their entire being has been reconstituted for life in a new world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 39, '39. Certain of the scribes. [The Great Commandment, Luke 20:39, 40=Matt. 22:34–40=Mark 12:28–34. Major comment: Mark.]');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 41, '41. He said unto them. [Jesus Silences His Critics, Luke 20:41–44=Matt. 22:41–46=Mark 12:35–37. Major comment: Matthew.]');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 42, '42. In the book of Psalms. Only Luke remarks that the quotation is from the book of Psalms.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 45, '45. In the audience. [Woes Upon Scribe and Pharisee, Luke 20:45–47=Matt. 23:1–39=Mark 12:38–40. Major comment: Matthew.] That is, “in the hearing”; in other words, while the scribes and the Pharisees were listening to Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 20, 0, 'ELLEN G. WHITE COMMENTS
  1–19 DA 593-600
  20–22 DA 601
  20–47 DA 601-609
  21 DA 602
  22–25 DA 725
  23–25 DA 602
  35, 36 GC 482; MM 101
  40 1T 57
  45–47 DA 610-620');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 1, '1. He looked up. [The Widow’s Mites, Luke 21:1–4=Mark 12:41–44. Major comment: Mark.]');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 5, '5. Some spake of the temple. [Signs of Christ’s Return, Luke 21:5–38=Matt. 24:1–51=Mark 13:1–37. Major comment: Matthew.]
Gifts. Probably “gifts” made to beautify the Temple, such as the golden vine of Herod at its entrance (Josephus Antiquities xv. 11. 3 cf. DA 575).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 7, '7. Shall come to pass. Literally, “are about to become [happen].”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 9, '9. By and by. Gr. eutheōs, “immediately,” or “at once.” The English expression “by and by” formerly had this meaning, but today has precisely the opposite meaning (see on ch. 17:7). Thus Jesus actually said, “the end is not at once.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 12, '12. Before all these. Luke here includes (vs. 12–16) a portion of the Olivet discourse not given by Matthew, probably because the latter had already reported practically the same line of thought, almost in the same words, from an earlier discourse. For comment on vs. 12–16 see on Matt. 10:17–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 13, '13. It shall turn to you. See on Mark 13:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 14, '14. Meditate. Gr. promeletaō, “to practice beforehand.” This probably refers to the going over of a speech beforehand in order that upon delivery the speaker might be more familiar with it. In making their defense before “kings and rulers” the disciples were not to have set speeches they could deliver by rote. For the reason back of Christ’s admonition see on Matt. 10:19, 20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 18, '18. Not an hair. This promise is not a universal guarantee of immunity from martyrdom, for Jesus had just said that some would be put to death (see v. 16). Perhaps it means here that rulers who hale Christians into court will have no power over them except when God shall permit (see John 19:11; Acts 5:35–38). Or, Christ’s words may be intended to refer to the ultimate result rather than to the immediate prospects of this world, and mean that the rulers of earth can have no power whatever over one’s eternal welfare (see John 10:28, 29; see on Matt. 10:28, 30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 19, '19. In your patience. Compare Matt. 24:13; Mark 13:13. The RSV reads, “By your endurance you will gain your lives,” a thought similar to that of Matt. 24:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 20, '20. Compassed with armies. That is, encircled by the Roman legions. For comment see on Matt. 24:2, 15–20.
Desolation thereof. The destruction of Jerusalem in A.D. 70 meant “the end” of the Jewish nation as such (see on Matt. 24:14, 15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 21, '21. Countries. That is, the country in contrast with the city. “Them which are in the midst of it [Jerusalem; see v. 20]” are city residents, and “them that are in the countries,” residents of the rural districts, living in the smaller towns and villages.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 22, '22. Days of vengeance. See on Matt. 23:35, 36.
Which are written. Evidently a reference to the curses for disobedience (see Deut. 27:11–26; 28:15–68).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 23, '23. Wrath upon this people. That is, upon the Jews. See on Matt. 23:35; cf. Jer. 5:29. For a full discussion of God’s plan for Israel, and of their failure and rejection as a nation, see Vol. IV, pp. 26-30.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 24, '24. Edge of the sword. Literally, “mouth of the sword.” Obviously this refers to the bloody climax of the siege of Jerusalem in the year A.D. 70 (see pp. 70-77; see on Matt. 24:2, 15–20).
Led away captive. As Moses had predicted, if Israel would “not observe to do all the words of this law that are written in this book” (Deut. 28:58, 63–68). This warning had already been fulfilled previously, in the Babylonian captivity (Jer. 16:13; 40:1, 2; 52:12–16, 28–31; Dan. 1:1–3; 9:11–14; etc.). In connection with Gabriel’s explanation to Daniel concerning the eventual restoration from Babylonian captivity (see on Dan. 9:24, 25), there was also coupled a warning that repetition of the mistakes that occasioned the Babylonian captivity would result in a second destruction of Jerusalem and the Temple (see on Dan. 9:26, 27). It was to this second destruction and the scattering of the Jews that Christ here refers (see on Matt. 24:15–20; cf. Luke 21:20). This situation was not to be remedied “until the times of the Gentiles be fulfilled.” For further comment see Vol. IV, pp. 30-36.
Trodden down. The semblance of autonomy the Jews enjoyed under Roman jurisdiction prior to A.D. 70 was never restored, and ever since that fateful year Jerusalem has remained largely, if not entirely, under Gentile control. As a result of the revolt under Bar Cocheba, crushed in A.D. 135, all Jews were forbidden to enter the city on pain of death. Never since that time has Jerusalem been a truly Jewish city. The Temple was never rebuilt after A.D. 70. Among others, Romans, Saracens, Norsemen, Turks, Crusaders, and Arabs have');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 25, '25. Signs. See on Matt. 24:29.
With perplexity. The Greek implies that the “perplexity” is due to “the sea and the waves roaring.” The last part of v. 25 reads literally, “and upon the earth distress of nations in perplexity [at] the roaring of the sea and the waves.”
The sea. Christ here associates manifestations of the destructive forces of nature with signs in the heavens immediately preceding His return to earth in power and glory.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 26, '26. Men’s hearts …. Literally, “men expiring from fear,” or “men fainting from fear.” The latter part of the verse gives as the climactic reason for men’s hearts failing them for fear, the shaking of “the powers of heaven.” The scene here depicted takes place under the seventh plague (EW 41; GC 636). Upon this scene “the wicked look with terror and amazement” (GC 636), calling for the mountains and the rocks to fall upon them (Rev. 6:14–17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 29, '29. All the trees. Luke informs readers unfamiliar with the fig tree that the truth thus illustrated applies to all trees, not to the fig only.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 31, '31. The kingdom of God. That is, the kingdom of glory in contrast with the kingdom of divine grace (see on Matt. 4:17; 5:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 34, '34. Surfeiting. Gr. kraipalē, “intoxication,” or “a drunken headache,” from kras, “head,” and pallō, “to sway,” or “to toss about.” Greek medical writers used kraipalē in reference to the nausea and stupor following a drunken debauch.
Cares. That is, “anxieties,” “worries.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 35, '35. Snare. See 1 Thess. 5:4; 1 Tim. 3:7; 2 Tim. 2:26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 36, '36. Watch. Gr. agrupneō, “to be sleepless,” literally, “keep awake.”
Pray always. See on ch. 18:1.
Accounted worthy to escape. Important textual evidence may be cited (cf. p. 146) for reading “prevail to escape,” or “have strength to escape.”
Stand before. See on ch. 19:24. This is the supreme goal of the Christian life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 37, '37. In the day time. A summary statement, in retrospect, upon Jesus’ activities during the first three days of the passion week (see on Matt. 23:38).
At night. Sunday and Monday nights Jesus had returned to Bethany (see on Mark 11:11, 12, 20; see also DA 581). This night, Tuesday, Jesus and the disciples probably spent on the Mount of Olives.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 38, '38. All the people. This is probably a summary statement also, comparable with that of v. 37. Jesus did not teach in the Temple after this time.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista V', 'Lucas', 21, 0, 'ELLEN G. WHITE COMMENTS
  3 DA 615, 616; GW 467
  5–38 DA 627-636
